A||||5||Ferrari355||4 cyl to V6 conversion questions!!!||||4||
Z||000000||Ferrari355||05-09-2000||05:17 PM||||Ok, we are wanting to take the 4 cyl out of our 84 SE auto and put the V6 in it but my dad is wondering about the wiring harness'. Is there going to be any major changes to get this to work? if so what all are we going to need to do to get the V6 to work. And, where is the relay to the decklid latch located. cause we installed the switch and it has the wires but where is the relay? Thanks, TTYL...<P>           -ALEX...||||reg||4
Z||000001||DRH||05-09-2000||07:14 PM||||I think there is a <B>major</B> difference in the 84 harness and all others. On the 84 a big connector that connects most wires in the engine bay except the ECM wires will be mounted on the firewall next to the ECM wires. On 85-88 that connector is mounted near the battery. You will probably have to do some <B>careful</B> splicing or maybe get <B>all</B> wiring from the V6 car? Anybody here ever done this???<P>Also remember hearing the 84 decklid release is wired different... not sure how though. The relay is under the dash just to the right of the steering column on newer ones, not sure about 84.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Rodrv6||05-09-2000||08:29 PM||||DRH is right.  The '84 models are the hardest ones to put a V-6 into because they never came that way and several things will need to be considered. The wiring harness is completely different.  Not just the one in the engine compartment, but also the main harness in the car.  The biggest problem is the location of the large plug that the engine harness plugs into.  If you try to use the V-6 harness you'll discover it's a couple of feet short of hooking up to the 84 plug.  Also the tail light harness is different on the 85 and later cars.  On the 84 it goes from the same large plug as the engine harness and snakes around the left side of the car back to the tail lights. On 85 and later cars the tail light harnesss hooks into the same plug as the engine harness, but it is way over on the right side near the battery, so the tail light harness is routed under the right side back to the tail lights.  The easiest solution is probably to use the entire wire harness out of a V-6 Fiero to convert an 84.<BR>The trunk release on the 84 did not use any kind of relay, just sent power straight to the solonoid on the decklid.  This is according to my 84 shop manual and also checking my black 84.<BR>I hope I don't sound like I'm trying to discourage you from doing this swap, but you need to know what you may be up against. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000003||Oreif||05-10-2000||11:17 AM||||As for 84's yes they are difficult to swap in a 2.8L. I have heard doing a 3800 or V-8 swap is easier than trying to put in all the stock items for a 2.8!||||reg||1
Z||000004||coyote||05-10-2000||12:45 PM|||| you will also have to drop the fuel tank and change the pump.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ferrari355||05-11-2000||12:11 PM||||Thanks for the info, it's as I suspected.<BR>The 86 2.8 with 4 speed is for sale now.<BR>$200. for the engine or both.<BR>      bks854@aol.com  in okla.||||reg||1
